    {
      "tag": "main",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "h1",
          "children": [
            "Document models and distributed-system decisions"
          ]
        },
        "\n",
        {
          "tag": "p",
          "children": [
            "Meetings 29–30. Build on the existing ",
            {
              "tag": "a",
              "attrs": {
                "href": "../nosql/mongodb.html"
              },
              "children": [
                "MongoDB shop lab"
              ]
            },
            " and MongoDB lecture. Distribution is a short application of existing material, not a new cluster-administration unit."
          ]
        },
        "\n",
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "children": [
            "Meeting 29: modeling and queries"
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "ol",
          "children": [
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Complete the shop dataset setup and CRUD exercises in the existing lab."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Model an order and its line items once with embedding and once with references. State what happens if a product price changes after checkout."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Write a filter for completed orders and an aggregation by customer using the lab’s actual field names."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Map an order/customer/product relationship back to relational tables. Compare join needs, validation, update frequency, and bounded versus unbounded arrays."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Explain why “schema flexible” does not mean the application has no schema."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "\n",
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "children": [
            "Meeting 30: scenarios (100 minutes)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "p",
          "children": [
            "10 review, 15 worked replica-lag example, 60 small-group decisions, 15 defense."
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "ol",
          "children": [
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                {
                  "tag": "strong",
                  "children": [
                    "Replica lag:"
                  ]
                },
                " a customer changes an address on the primary at 10:00:00, then reads from a replica two seconds behind. Predict the result and propose read-your-writes behavior."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                {
                  "tag": "strong",
                  "children": [
                    "Last ticket during a partition:"
                  ]
                },
                " two regions each believe one ticket remains and cannot communicate. Compare accepting both requests, rejecting/deferring one region, and reserving disjoint inventory in advance. State the invariant and cost of each choice."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                {
                  "tag": "strong",
                  "children": [
                    "Catalog versus checkout:"
                  ]
                },
                " stale catalog descriptions may be tolerated, but charging twice is not. Specify different consistency/idempotency needs for the two operations."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                {
                  "tag": "strong",
                  "children": [
                    "Analytics:"
                  ]
                },
                " explain why a reporting replica or warehouse can reduce transactional load and what freshness tradeoff follows."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                {
                  "tag": "strong",
                  "children": [
                    "CAP precision:"
                  ]
                },
                " during a network partition, explain the conflict between linearizable consistency and responding to every request. Do not describe CAP as an unconditional “pick any two” menu."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "\n",
        {
          "tag": "p",
          "children": [
            {
              "tag": "strong",
              "children": [
                "Submit:"
              ]
            },
            " a two-page decision memo with assumptions, failure outcomes, and reconciliation rules. ",
            {
              "tag": "strong",
              "children": [
                "Stretch:"
              ]
            },
            " sketch a two-phase-commit coordinator failure and explain blocking without implementing a cluster."
          ]
        },
        "\n"
      ]
    },
    "\n"
  ]
}
